/*
 * EmitsInit.ag — §7.49's initializer, as emitted.
 *
 * Copyright © 2026 Kyle J. Cardoza. MIT licence (see docs/LICENSE).
 *
 * §7.49 gives `init` its own rules, and every one of them is the EMITTER's
 * rather than the language's: the `method` keyword and the `-> Self` return are
 * implied and unwritten, and the body writes no `return` at all. So an
 * initializer is the one declaration whose emitted form carries content the
 * author never wrote, which is exactly what a golden has to pin.
 *
 * Both rules were missing, and the result was a silent wrong answer rather than
 * a loud one: `init(n: Int32) { … }` emitted
 *
 *     - (void)init:(int32_t)n { … }
 *
 * — a method in the `init` family, so clang accepted it and only warned — and
 * the `- (instancetype)init(n:)` that every `Class(n: 3)` call is a send to did
 * not exist at all. Nothing but looking at the emitted text could have caught
 * it, which is what this file is for.
 *
 * `Point2` is a ROOT class on purpose. §7.49's subclass chain
 * (`self = Superclass()`, emitting `self = [super init]`) is a separate rule with
 * its own emission, and a specimen that needed it would assert two things at
 * once.
 */
class Point2: Object {
    property x: Int32
    property y: Int32

    init(x: Int32, y: Int32) {
        self.x = x;
        self.y = y;
    }

    method sum() -> Int32 {
        return self.x + self.y;
    }
}
